A tree is symmetric when the left side of one pair matches the right side of the other pair. The helper checks two nodes at the same time, compares their values, and then checks the outside pair and inside pair.

For example, if the first pair has values 2 and 2, the next comparisons are left-left with right-right and left-right with right-left.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) : data(val), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {
private:
    bool areMirrors(TreeNode* first, TreeNode* second) {
        if (first == nullptr || second == nullptr) {
            return first == second;
        }

        if (first->data != second->data) {
            return false;
        }

        return areMirrors(first->left, second->right) &&
               areMirrors(first->right, second->left);
    }

public:
    bool isSymmetric(TreeNode* root) {
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return true;
        }

        return areMirrors(root->left, root->right);
    }
};
```

The empty tree and a tree with only a root are both symmetric. The recursion uses space for the tree height, so a very deep tree could make the call stack large.

Time complexity: O(n), where n is the number of nodes.
Space complexity: O(h), where h is the tree height.

Here the tree is read one level at a time. For each level, null markers are kept in the list so missing children are not lost. A level is symmetric when it equals its reversed copy.

The root level has only the root and does not affect the result. After that, the next level is built from the current nodes. For example, values `[2, 2]` pass, while `[2, 3]` fail right away.

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) : data(val), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

#include <queue>
#include <vector>

class Solution {
public:
    bool isSymmetric(TreeNode* root) {
        if (root == nullptr) {
            return true;
        }

        std::queue<TreeNode*> currentLevel;
        currentLevel.push(root);

        while (!currentLevel.empty()) {
            int levelSize = static_cast<int>(currentLevel.size());
            std::vector<int> values;
            values.reserve(levelSize * 2);

            for (int i = 0; i < levelSize; ++i) {
                TreeNode* node = currentLevel.front();
                currentLevel.pop();

                if (node == nullptr) {
                    values.push_back(1000000007);
                    continue;
                }

                values.push_back(node->data);
                currentLevel.push(node->left);
                currentLevel.push(node->right);
            }

            for (int left = 0, right = static_cast<int>(values.size()) - 1;
                 left < right;
                 ++left, --right) {
                if (values[left] != values[right]) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

The null marker must be different from any possible node value. This code uses `1000000007`, so it assumes that value is outside the allowed node range. Keeping nulls makes cases with missing children work correctly.

Time complexity: O(n), with each real node visited once.
Space complexity: O(n) for the queue and level values.

This version stores nodes in pairs. For every pair removed from the queue, the values must match. The children are added in mirror order: first's left with second's right, then first's right with second's left.

A small dry run for a root with children 2 and 2 starts with the pair (2, 2). If their children are 3 and 3 on the outside, that pair is checked next. A missing child is also important, because one null child beside a real child means the tree is not symmetric.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null; right = null; }
 * }
 **/

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Queue;

class Solution {
    public boolean isSymmetric(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return true;
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();
        queue.offer(root.left);
        queue.offer(root.right);

        while (!queue.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode first = queue.poll();
            TreeNode second = queue.poll();

            if (first == null && second == null) {
                continue;
            }
            if (first == null || second == null || first.data != second.data) {
                return false;
            }

            queue.offer(first.left);
            queue.offer(second.right);
            queue.offer(first.right);
            queue.offer(second.left);
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

The queue may contain nodes from different sides of the tree at the same time, but each two entries always form one mirror pair. The root itself does not need to be compared with another node.

Time complexity: O(n).
Space complexity: O(w), where w is the largest number of nodes held at one level.

Two stacks can copy the recursive mirror check without using the call stack. One stack walks the left side in normal order, while the other walks the matching right side in the opposite order.

The dry run is simple: push the two root children. After matching them, push left-left with right-right and left-right with right-left. Every pair must have the same value and the same null status.

```java
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null; right = null; }
 * }
 **/

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

class Solution {
    public boolean isSymmetric(TreeNode root) {
        if (root == null) {
            return true;
        }

        Deque<TreeNode> leftStack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Deque<TreeNode> rightStack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        leftStack.push(root.left);
        rightStack.push(root.right);

        while (!leftStack.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode leftNode = leftStack.pop();
            TreeNode rightNode = rightStack.pop();

            if (leftNode == null && rightNode == null) {
                continue;
            }
            if (leftNode == null || rightNode == null || leftNode.data != rightNode.data) {
                return false;
            }

            leftStack.push(leftNode.left);
            leftStack.push(leftNode.right);
            rightStack.push(rightNode.right);
            rightStack.push(rightNode.left);
        }

        return true;
    }
}
```

An empty tree and a root with no children return `true`. This does not change the input tree, and it avoids recursive calls, which can help when the tree is very tall.

Time complexity: O(n).
Space complexity: O(h) for the two stacks, with O(n) possible in a one-sided tree.

# Intuition

A normal level order traversal is not sufficient to determine whether a binary tree is symmetric because it loses information about missing nodes. Two different tree structures can produce the same level order traversal if null positions are ignored.

To preserve the exact structure of the tree, I enqueue `nullptr` whenever a child is missing. While processing each level:
- If the node is valid, I store its value.
- If the node is `nullptr`, I store a sentinel value (`101`). Since the problem guarantees `-100 <= Node.val <= 100`, `101` uniquely represents a missing node.

After constructing each level, symmetry reduces to checking whether that level forms a palindrome.

The final level always contains only sentinel values (all missing nodes), so it is removed before performing the palindrome checks.

# Approach

1. Perform a level order traversal (BFS).
2. Whenever a child is absent, push `nullptr` into the queue to preserve the tree structure.
3. While processing a level:
   - Store the node value if the node exists.
   - Store `101` if the node is `nullptr`.
4. Remove the final level consisting entirely of sentinel values.
5. For every remaining level, use two pointers to check whether it is a palindrome.
6. If every level is symmetric, return `true`; otherwise return `false`.

# Complexity

- **Time complexity:** `O(n)`

  Each node (and its corresponding null placeholders) is processed once, and the total work done by all palindrome checks is linear in the total number of stored elements.

- **Space complexity:** `O(n)`

  The queue used for BFS and the level-order storage together require linear extra space.

# Code

```cpp
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *     TreeNode() : val(0), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 *     TreeNode(int x) : val(x), left(nullptr), right(nullptr) {}
 *     TreeNode(int x, TreeNode *left, TreeNode *right) : val(x), left(left), right(right) {}
 * };
 */
class Solution {
private:
    void levelorder(TreeNode* root , vector<vector<int>>& ans){
        if(root == nullptr) return;

        queue<TreeNode*> q;
        q.push(root);

        while(!q.empty()){
            int size = q.size();
            vector<int> level;

            for(int i = 0; i < size; i++){
                TreeNode* node = q.front();
                q.pop();

                if(node == nullptr){
                    level.push_back(101);
                    continue;
                }

                level.push_back(node->val);

                if(node->left) q.push(node->left);
                else q.push(nullptr);

                if(node->right) q.push(node->right);
                else q.push(nullptr);
            }

            ans.push_back(level);
        }

        ans.pop_back();
    }

public:
    bool isSymmetric(TreeNode* root) {
        vector<vector<int>> ans;
        levelorder(root, ans);

        for(auto &level : ans){
            int l = 0;
            int r = level.size() - 1;

            while(l <= r){
                if(level[l] != level[r]){
                    return false;
                }
                l++;
                r--;
            }
        }

        return true;
    }
};
```

## Intuition
A binary tree is symmetric if its left and right subtrees are mirror images of each other.

So, for every pair of nodes:

Their values must be equal.
root1->left must mirror root2->right.
root1->right must mirror root2->left.

## Approach
Compare the left and right subtree of the root.
If both nodes are NULL, they are symmetric.
If only one is NULL, they are not symmetric.
If their values differ, return false.
Recursively compare:
root1->right with root2->left
root1->left with root2->right
Both comparisons must be true.

## Solution
```cpp[]
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode *left;
 *     TreeNode *right;
 *      TreeNode(int val) : data(val) , left(nullptr) , right(nullptr) {}
 * };
 **/

class Solution {

bool check(TreeNode* root1 , TreeNode* root2){
    if(root1 == NULL && root2== NULL) return true;
    if(root1 && root2== NULL) return false;
    if(root2 && root1== NULL) return false;
    if(root1->data != root2->data) return false;

    bool check1 = check(root1->right , root2->left);
    bool check2 = check(root1->left , root2->right);

    if(!check1 || !check2) return false;
    return true;
}

public:
    bool isSymmetric(TreeNode* root) {
        return check(root->left , root->right);
    }
};
```

## Complexity
**Time Complexity:**
O(N) — Each node is visited once while comparing the two subtrees.

**Space Complexity:**
O(H) — Due to the recursion stack, where H is the height of the tree.53:[
